    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4623",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256025/assets/f918ad328cb4eda8ff2522e340e83d717fef04bf4412604ce9f7bcac8372e60c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=2\\sin{(x+C)},$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\pi <        C <        \\pi,$ what is the value of $C?$",
      "worked_solution": "The constant $C$ is the phase shift of the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256025/assets/45e663c28deb060417e3c31020f63f7eef565c1f49da7e662f00304b6c7fd3e2.png)\n\nTo plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sin x.$\n\n- Vertically stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ to give $y = 2 \\sin x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $2$ units **right** to give $y=2\\sin \\left(x-2\\right).$\n\nTherefore, $C=-2.$",
